/**
 * THE ICON SET. Drawn artwork, imported — not composed from primitives.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HOW A GLYPH GETS HERE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 *   1. Generate a ROW of related icons in ONE image (`openai_hazel`, quality
 *      high). One sheet is one visual decision, and that is what makes a set
 *      agree with itself; icons generated one at a time do not share a weight,
 *      a terminal treatment or a light direction, and the drift cannot be
 *      fixed afterwards.
 *   2. `node scripts/split-icon-row.mjs sheet.png <count>` — per-icon crops.
 *   3. `magick` crop, resize, BLUR, threshold, pad to a square of `max(w,h)`.
 *      Blur before threshold, or potrace traces the pixel staircase.
 *   4. `potrace -s --alphamax 1.0 --opttolerance 0.6 -t 12`
 *   5. `node scripts/flatten-svg-path.mjs <traced.svg> <keyline>`
 *   6. `node scripts/svg-to-glyph.mjs <flat.svg> <name> <keyline>`
 *   7. `npm run icons:lint`, then LOOK at it in `/kit` at 16, 24 and 64.
 *
 * Step 7 is not optional and the two halves are not interchangeable. The lint
 * catches a glyph that disagrees with the system; only the eye catches a glyph
 * that was silently cropped, which has happened once and passed every rule.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY NO COMPOSED GLYPH REMAINS
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * There used to be eighteen, assembled from the `taper`/`chamfer`/`shield`
 * vocabulary in `system.ts`. They satisfied every rule the linter enforces and
 * were dead on the page: a set built from eight primitives cannot reach the
 * 70-370 coordinates a drawn icon needs. They were replaced rather than kept
 * alongside, because two icon languages in one set is worse than either alone.
 *
 * That vocabulary stays in `system.ts` — it is still right for a purely
 * geometric mark — but nothing here uses it, and a new glyph should be drawn
 * and imported rather than assembled.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The nine tab glyphs stay in `TabIcon.tsx`, and `Hearts.tsx` keeps its own
 * three-state renderer. Those are components with behaviour — focus states,
 * clip-path compositing, per-scheme steel — rather than artwork, and folding
 * them in here would trade working code for a redraw.
 */
import type { Glyph } from './system';

/**
 * THE ECONOMY AND CONTENT MARKS. Cut from one generated sheet, so the five
 * agree with each other by construction rather than by luck.
 *
 * THESE ARE THE KEYLINES DOING THEIR JOB. A gem is a diamond and takes the
 * `diagonal` keyline, so it is drawn WIDER than the square-keyline marks beside
 * it. Set them all to one size and the gem reads small — the failure the
 * keyline exists to prevent, and one impossible to name by eye until you see
 * the row together.
 *
 * `card-badge` is on the SQUARE keyline, not portrait, and that was a
 * correction the lint forced: the card is drawn tilted, which makes it wider
 * than an upright card and 112% of the portrait keyline. The drawing was right;
 * the classification was wrong.
 *
 * `shop` carries seven paths because the awning is scalloped. It is the most
 * complex mark in the set and the first to check if the row ever stops holding
 * up at 16pt.
 */
